<section id="book-history" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-8">
  <div class="text-center mb-10">
    <p class="font-cinzel text-gold-dark text-xs tracking-[0.4em] uppercase mb-3">
      Chapter IV
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-cinzel text-navy text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-4">
      The Story of the Book
    </h2>
    <p class="font-garamond text-brown text-lg md:text-xl max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
      The treatise itself has a history worthy of its message: written, buried,
      forgotten, and found again more than a century later.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="text-center mb-12">
    <.sacred_divider class="max-w-[210px] mx-auto" />
    <blockquote class="font-garamond italic text-xl md:text-2xl text-navy leading-[1.7] max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      "I clearly foresee that raging beasts will come in fury to tear to pieces with
      their diabolical teeth this little book and the one whom the Holy Ghost has made
      use of to write it, or at least to smother it in the darkness and silence of a
      coffer, in order that it may not appear."
    </blockquote>
    <p class="font-garamond italic text-sm text-brown-light max-w-2xl mx-auto mt-4">
      True Devotion to the Blessed Virgin, n. 114 &mdash; written some eighty years
      before the manuscript vanished into a chest during the French Revolution
    </p>
    <.sacred_divider cross={false} class="max-w-[120px] mx-auto opacity-60" />
  </div>

  <ol class="relative border-l border-gold/30 ml-4 md:ml-8 space-y-8">
    <li class="relative pl-8 md:pl-10">
      <span
        class="absolute -left-[0.5625rem] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-gold border-2 border-parchment"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
      </span>
      <p class="font-cinzel text-gold-dark text-xl mb-1">c. 1712</p>
      <h3 class="font-cinzel text-navy text-2xl mb-2">The treatise is written</h3>
      <p class="font-garamond text-brown text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
        During his missions in western France, St. Louis-Marie Grignion de Montfort
        sets down the summit of his Marian teaching in a small manuscript, without
        a title page, four years before his death in 1716.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="relative pl-8 md:pl-10">
      <span
        class="absolute -left-[0.5625rem] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-gold border-2 border-parchment"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
      </span>
      <p class="font-cinzel text-gold-dark text-xl mb-1">1790s</p>
      <h3 class="font-cinzel text-navy text-2xl mb-2">Hidden during the Revolution</h3>
      <p class="font-garamond text-brown text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
        As the French Revolution rages and religious houses are searched, the
        missionaries of the Company of Mary entrust their books and papers to
        neighbouring farmers, who bury them in chests. The manuscript survives,
        returns to the motherhouse with the rest, and passes out of memory on
        a shelf.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="relative pl-8 md:pl-10">
      <span
        class="absolute -left-[0.5625rem] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-gold border-2 border-parchment"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
      </span>
      <p class="font-cinzel text-gold-dark text-xl mb-1">April 22, 1842</p>
      <h3 class="font-cinzel text-navy text-2xl mb-2">The manuscript is found</h3>
      <p class="font-garamond text-brown text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
        Fr. Rautureau, librarian of the motherhouse at Saint-Laurent-sur-Sevre,
        comes upon the dusty, untitled manuscript on its shelf. It is recognized
        at once as the saint's own hand and published the following year, in
        1843.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="relative pl-8 md:pl-10">
      <span
        class="absolute -left-[0.5625rem] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-gold border-2 border-parchment"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
      </span>
      <p class="font-cinzel text-gold-dark text-xl mb-1">1862</p>
      <h3 class="font-cinzel text-navy text-2xl mb-2">Translated into English</h3>
      <p class="font-garamond text-brown text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
        Fr. Frederick William Faber, the Oratorian hymn-writer and convert, gives
        the treatise its classic English translation, the version still most widely
        read today.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="relative pl-8 md:pl-10">
      <span
        class="absolute -left-[0.5625rem] top-1.5 w-4 h-4 rounded-full bg-gold border-2 border-parchment"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
      </span>
      <p class="font-cinzel text-gold-dark text-xl mb-1">July 20, 1947</p>
      <h3 class="font-cinzel text-navy text-2xl mb-2">The author is canonized</h3>
      <p class="font-garamond text-brown text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
        Pope Pius XII canonizes Louis-Marie Grignion de Montfort, and the little
        book that raging beasts could not destroy goes on to shape the piety of
        millions, among them a young Karol Wojtyla.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>
